  "correct": "F",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Fix the quadrant first",
      "body": "<p>The given interval covers quadrants III and IV. Tangent is positive where sine and cosine have the same sign.</p>",
      "recap": "The interval and tangent sign determine the quadrant.",
      "pitfall": "A positive tangent does not force a positive cosine.",
      "pause": "Which of these two quadrants has positive tangent?",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l15",
          "reminder": "Tangent is sine divided by cosine; signs and the permitted interval matter."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Find the magnitude",
      "body": "<p>Use an acute reference triangle with opposite side 2 and adjacent side 1, so that its tangent is 2.</p>",
      "recap": "A reference triangle gives magnitudes before quadrant signs.",
      "pitfall": "The adjacent side is 1; do not use the opposite side for cosine.",
      "pause": "Find the hypotenuse by Pythagoras.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l15",
          "reminder": "Cosine is adjacent divided by hypotenuse for the reference angle."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Combine magnitude and quadrant",
      "body": "Use adjacent divided by hypotenuse in your reference triangle. Then assign the sign using the quadrant you identified.",
      "recap": "The reference triangle supplies a magnitude; the original angle supplies its sign.",
      "pitfall": "Rationalising a denominator must preserve the value.",
      "pause": "Calculate the signed cosine and match it to an option.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l16",
          "reminder": "The reference triangle supplies a magnitude; the original angle supplies its sign."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02218;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msup><mn>360</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02218;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow></math> lies in quadrant III: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02218;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msup><mn>270</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02218;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>A reference triangle with opposite side 2 and adjacent side 1 has hypotenuse <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p><p>Restoring the negative sign gives <strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>: F.</strong></p>",
